                     #  # Cannot Log In / Account Issues # #


    Initial check:
Wrong password or username
Caps Lock / Num Lock enabled
Temporary profile glitch

-If possible, log in with another user to isolate the issue


    Secondary Check/cause:
Corrupted user profile
Account disabled or locked
Network issues for domain accounts

Windows + R → control userpasswords2
Verify the account exists and is enabled (Should show if disabled or not)

    Steps / Resolution:
Check user accounts
Run: control userpasswords2 
Verify the account exists and is enabled


        Reset password (if needed)
- For local accounts: reset through Settings → Accounts → Sign-in options
- For Microsoft accounts: reset via account.microsoft.com
3. Check for domain issues (if applicable)
Ensure PC is connected to the network/domain
Try logging in with another domain account to verify connectivity
4. Temporary profile fix
If logged into a temporary profile, sign out and restart
Check C:\Users for the real profile folder

        Escalation
Cause (advanced/root cause):
Corrupted registry for the user profile
Domain/Active Directory misconfiguration (for work PCs)
Permissions issues



      Steps / Resolution:
Repair corrupted user profile

Log in with another admin account, back up the user profile, create a new account, restore files, and check registry profile issues. 
If this is a school/work Computer you’d have to  escalate to IT and they'd follow those steps because they would have access C:\Users


Steps will take IT: Log in with another admin account
Navigate to C:\Users[corrupted_profile] and back up important files
Create a new account and copy files to the new profile


Check registry for profile issues


Open Registry Editor → HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList


Look for .bak entries or incorrect paths for the user profile
.bak only appears if Windows failed to load a user profile correctly.
Usually after a login failure: Windows creates a new temporary profile Renames the old one with .bak



If a .bak entry is found, would backup/Export the the .bak registry,
Do NOT edit  and or delete directly without backing up
Right-click the .bak folder → Export → save somewhere safe
Rename the temporary profile, instead of deleting keeps temp folders backup in case 
Remove .bak from the Correct profile, and ensure the ProfileImagePath points to the correct user folder.
-> if it doesn't solve the issue you have a real corrupt profile. back up computer to external drive 
Check permissions
Right-click user folder → Properties → Security
Ensure the user has full control


          Escalation
If a domain account cannot log in, report it to IT and give them the Event Viewer logs for authentication errors
use Active Directory (AD) / Directory Manager to handle the account to:

Check account status (disabled, expired, or permissions).
Unlock the account if it’s locked due to too many failed logins.
Force profile/replication updates if needed for login.
Reset the password if the user forgot it.
View logs from the domain controller to diagnose authentication issues.
